WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.060
If you’re in a theater during previews,
and you see this ….

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:07.020
… you know to expect a comedy movie.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:09.050
If you see this — you know it’s going
to be action.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:11.590
Make the letters a little skinny — and boom.

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:12.930
It’s sci-fi.

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:14.620
You know this one's going to have fast
cars...

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:17.279
This one’s going to have Michael Cera….
… and this one’s gonna be a rom-com.

00:00:17.279 --> 00:00:19.789
You don’t even need the music to know it.

00:00:19.789 --> 00:00:20.840
But what about this?

00:00:22.269 --> 00:00:27.220
This typeface — Trajan – is probably one
of the most popular movie poster fonts ever.

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:30.820
You can see it in Crouching Tiger, Hidden
Dragon, Double Jeopardy, Letters from Iwo Jima,

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:36.640
It’s everywhere.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:37.780
So how did it get there?

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:46.900
I'm Yves Peters, I am a graphic designer who
has been writing professionally about type

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:49.740
and typography since about 10 years.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:51.460
Yves has looked at a lot of posters.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:54.160
And a few years ago, he started to notice
a trend.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:57.710
I started clicking, clicking, clicking, and
didn't know where I started because I ended

00:00:57.710 --> 00:01:00.120
up looking at about 16,000 posters.

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:03.340
Yeah.

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:10.380
Well I look at about 100 posters per month and
I've been doing this series since 2006…

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:13.700
It’s a lot of posters every year.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.000
He noticed that the use of Trajan rose significantly
in the last couple of decades.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:20.620
But why?

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:26.880
In 1989, designer Carol Twombly adapted inscriptions
from Roman emperor Trajan’s column into

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:28.380
a digital typeface.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:32.009
It was made for Adobe, so it was a part of
the software that a lot of people were starting

00:01:32.009 --> 00:01:33.680
to use to make posters.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:37.920
A couple years later, it made its movie poster
debut here — for Héctor Babenco’s

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:39.800
At Play in the Fields of the Lord.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:42.930
After that, it was on The Bodyguard, and Scent
of a Woman.

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:45.969
Then it was on three 1993 box office hits.

00:01:45.969 --> 00:01:48.400
By 1994, it was everywhere.

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:55.850
It's originally used for epic movies, movies
about people that overcome difficulties, like

00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:58.600
the big war epics and so on.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:04.000
But then you see gradually that it becomes
the standard movie font.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.060
It's like the Arial of movie posters.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:10.290
So people need to churn out a poster really
quickly, and they just pick Trajan.

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:15.170
Eventually, that overuse changed the kinds
of movies that used the typeface.

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:19.560
You don’t see it as much on the big productions
anymore.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:26.000
It's become the typeface of horror movies,
B-movies, and also the straight-to-video ones.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:32.310
So you'll see that a lot of posters are for
the lesser movies that want to pretend they

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:34.220
are better than they actually are.

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:39.880
The rise and fall of Trajan illustrates the
downside of digital typeface.

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:45.170
The convenience of swapping fonts is a huge
shift from old movie poster days when hand-painted

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:49.760
typefaces or "lettering" was an art that was
specific to each movie.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:54.730
So what you see behind me — this is more
like the pre-blockbuster thing...

00:02:54.730 --> 00:03:00.900
pre-genres, pre-standard styles — and
that's why you will be hard pressed to find

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:02.280
two that are similar.

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:07.860
That doesn’t make today’s genre-specific
typography bad design.

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:12.230
These visual clichés make it possible to
communicate tone and plot details to an audience

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:13.790
incredibly efficiently.

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:15.470
And there’s a certain art to that.

00:03:15.470 --> 00:03:19.400
People get nostalgic about the old era of
movie posters, when everything was still done

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:20.671
by hand and still very unique.

00:03:20.671 --> 00:03:26.960
But you could compare it to music, where some
people say, “nothing good was produced since

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:27.960
the Beatles.”

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:30.770
But then there are people who say there’s
nothing good since Mozart.

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:35.000
Fair enough, but there's many other interesting
things that are being produced now.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:36.900
There’s just much more.

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:40.860
But there's still a lot of very good poster
design happening.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:43.760
It’s just, you need to know where to look.”

